{
  "item_id": "PRISMA_2020_3",
  "elements": [
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e1",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "The combination of systemic activation of coagulation, extensive pulmonary endotheliopathy and thromboinflammatory process caused by SARS-CoV2 results in an increased risk of thrombotic complications, mainly occurring in the venous system [1, 2]. In hospitalized COVID-19 patients, the incidence of venous thromboembolism (VTE) appears to be considerably higher compared to the incidence reported in medical patients with infection, sepsis or septic shock [3]. This risk increases with disease severity, with an estimated incidence of VTE of 17% in non-critically ill COVID-19 patients and 24% in those admitted to intensive care units (ICUs) [3]. ... Available data suggested that, despite pharmacologic thromboprophylaxis, there is a substantial residual risk of VTE and a non-negligible risk of bleeding events [3, 5]. In addition, it remains unclear whether prophylactic or higher doses of anticoagulants may reduce in-hospital mortality in COVID-19 patients [6, 7].",
      "reason": "The introduction describes the current state (high VTE incidence, mechanisms, observational data) and highlights important uncertainties (residual VTE risk despite prophylaxis and unclear effects of higher doses)."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e2",
      "status": "PRESENT",
      "chunk_id": "text_4",
      "quote": "Recent randomized controlled trials (RCTs) comparing the efficacy and safety of high-dose (i.e., intermediate or therapeutic dose) with low-dose thromboprophylaxis in patients hospitalised with COVID-19 reported conflicting findings [8-13]. Although the primary objectives of these studies were to evaluate whether high-dose anticoagulation may prevent COVID-19 progression to organ failure and death, most trials reported data on VTE and bleeding events. The objective of this systematic review and meta-analysis was to evaluate the pooled efficacy and safety of high-dose versus low-dose thromboprophylaxis for VTE in hospitalized patients with COVID-19.",
      "reason": "The authors state that RCTs reported conflicting findings and explicitly state the objective and need to pool evidence to evaluate efficacy and safety, explaining the importance of the review."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e3",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "No text in the provided evidence discusses existing similar systematic reviews, the need for the current review relative to prior reviews, or cites/frames this work as an update or replication."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e4",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": "text_3",
      "quote": "The combination of systemic activation of coagulation, extensive pulmonary endotheliopathy and thromboinflammatory process caused by SARS-CoV2 results in an increased risk of thrombotic complications, mainly occurring in the venous system [1, 2].",
      "reason": "While the disease mechanism is described, the manuscript does not provide a brief description of the proposed mechanism of action of the intervention (how anticoagulant doses specifically act) in the provided text."
    },
    {
      "element_id": "prisma_2020_3_e5",
      "status": "MISSING",
      "chunk_id": null,
      "quote": null,
      "reason": "The provided evidence contains no logic model or discussion presenting hypothesised relationships between intervention components and outcomes for complex interventions or delivery contexts."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": 85
}

{
